  "step_3": "The decision reason requires assessing skewness, specifically whether a greater portion of probability is assigned to unfavorable outcomes (right-skew), and preferring the more right-skewed lottery. The individual does not mention skewness, probability distribution shape, or any comparison of the concentration of probability on unfavorable outcomes. Instead, they focus on the magnitude of outcomes and the probabilities of favorable outcomes (66% and 33%) and the acceptability of the 1% risk. The justification is based on expected value-like reasoning (high chance of same or better outcome) rather than on skewness. The information considered by the individual (probabilities of 2400 and 2500, risk of 0) is not aligned with the skewness criterion. The rule of the reason (prefer more right-skewed lottery) is not reflected in the justification (which emphasizes high probability of good outcomes and a small risk). Therefore, there is no evidence that the individual used the skewness-based reason. The individual's reasoning is fundamentally different in nature and focus.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
